    q: "A 53-year-old woman with a medical history notable for primary hypothyroidism presented 3 years ago with polyuria, polydipsia, and unintentional weight loss (13.2 lb [6 kg] over 2 months). At that time, laboratory tests documented a hemoglobin A1c value of 12.9% (117 mmol/mol), random glucose value of 367 mg/dL (20.4 mmol/L), and the absence of acidosis or ketosis. Her family history is positive for type 2 diabetes in her paternal grandfather and type 1 diabetes in her maternal second cousin. She follows a Mediterranean diet and exercises 6 days per week. She was initially started on metformin, 1000 mg twice daily. At first, she had mild improvement in her blood glucose, but she quickly required therapy intensification. Current medications are metformin, 1000 mg twice daily; empagliflozin, 25 mg daily; glimepiride, 4 mg daily; and insulin glargine, 15 units at bedtime (insulin was started 6 months ago). Her point-of-care hemoglobin A1c level decreased from 9.9% to 8.7% (85 to 72 mmol/mol) after starting insulin glargine.\n\nOn physical examination, her blood pressure is 119/67 mm Hg and pulse rate is 99 beats/min. Her height is 64.0 in (162.6 cm), and weight is 144.2 lb (65.4 kg) (BMI = 24.7 kg/m2).\n\nWhich of the following is the best next step in determining this patient’s most likely diagnosis?",
